## Common Workflows

### Investigate a Failed Build (MCP)
```
1. get_downstream_tree(build_url="<FAILED_URL>")             -- see job tree
2. get_pipeline_stages(job_path="<PATH>", build_number=<N>)  -- see which stages failed
3. analyze_pipeline(build_url="<FAILED_URL>")                -- root cause analysis
```

### Check Test Results (MCP)
```
1. get_test_results(job_path="CI-Jobs/e2e_ui_test_pipeline", build_number=42, mode="failures")
2. analyze_test_results(job_path="CI-Jobs/e2e_ui_test_pipeline", build_number=42, failures_only=True)
```

### Monitor a Running Build (CLI -- no MCP equivalent)
```bash
source ~/Documents/work/ai/tools/integrations/jenkins/jenkins-env.sh
jenkins-run monitor <BUILD_URL>
```

## Known Limitations

1. `analyze_pipeline` and `get_pipeline_stages` require Pipeline jobs (wfapi); FreeStyle jobs have limited data
2. SSL verification disabled by default (Red Hat internal CA)
3. `get_build_log` returns last 500 lines by default; use `start` param for pagination
4. Long-running monitoring uses CLI (`jenkins-run monitor`); MCP `get_build_status` is single-poll only
5. Specialized MCP tools (`analyze_pipeline`, `get_downstream_tree`, etc.) shell out to scripts under the directory in **`JENKINS_TOOLS_DIR`** (typically a clone of the **`jenkins-tools`** tree from [stolostron/acm-workflows](https://github.com/stolostron/acm-workflows) — not shipped under `ai/tools/`). Load credentials with **`integrations/jenkins/jenkins-env.sh`**; set **`JENKINS_TOOLS_DIR`** to that clone before using those MCP tools.

